PR Monitoring & Crisis Alert — n8n Workflow
Builder: Rodrigo Espinosa Quinteros
Type: n8n Automation Workflow
Status: Productized — sold and deployed across multiple clients with per-client adjustments
Trigger: Scheduled (every hour)
Domain: Public Relations, Brand Monitoring, Crisis Management

Overview
An automated PR monitoring pipeline that scans RSS news feeds hourly, uses AI to
assess sentiment and reputational risk for each mention, and routes high-risk items
to both a real-time Telegram alert and a CRM note in Odoo — all without human
intervention unless a crisis threshold is met.
The core value: a PR team is notified only when something actually requires their
attention. Low and medium risk mentions are logged silently; only high-risk mentions
trigger an alert. This eliminates the noise of manual monitoring while ensuring nothing
critical slips through.

Flow Architecture
[Cron Trigger - Every Hour]
        ↓
[RSS Feed Read]
        ↓
[Function - Normalize]
        ↓
[OpenAI - Sentiment & Risk Analysis]
        ↓
[IF: Risk = "alto" (high)?]
        ↓                    ↓
[Telegram Alert]     [Odoo - Create Note]
Total nodes: 7
Execution timeout: 60 minutes
Credentials required: OpenAI API, Telegram Bot, Odoo API
Environment variables: RSS_FEED_URL, TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID

Node Breakdown
1. Cron Trigger
Fires every hour on a fixed schedule. No external event required — the workflow
is self-initiating. This ensures continuous monitoring without relying on webhooks
or manual execution.
2. RSS Feed Read
Fetches the RSS feed defined in the RSS_FEED_URL environment variable. Using an
environment variable (rather than hardcoding the URL) makes this workflow portable
across clients — each deployment points to a different feed without touching the
workflow logic.
3. Function — Normalize
Standardizes the RSS output into a consistent data structure:
json{ "title": "", "content": "", "url": "", "source": "RSS" }
This normalization step is critical for downstream reliability. RSS feeds vary
significantly in structure across sources; normalizing before the AI step ensures
the prompt always receives clean, consistent input.
4. OpenAI — Sentiment & Risk Analysis
Sends each normalized item to GPT-4o-mini with a focused system prompt:

"Analyze brand mentions for PR. Return ONLY JSON with: {sentimiento, riesgo, resumen}."

Key design decisions:

JSON response format enforced — the node uses responseFormat: json, ensuring
the output is always machine-parseable for the downstream IF condition
GPT-4o-mini over GPT-4o — sufficient for classification tasks, significantly
lower cost per execution, important for a workflow that runs 24 times per day
Three-field output only — sentiment, risk level, and summary. No extraneous
data that would require additional parsing

5. IF — Risk Filter
Routes items based on the riesgo field returned by OpenAI:

riesgo = "alto" → triggers both output branches
Anything else → workflow ends silently (item is logged but no alert sent)

This is the core triage logic. The IF node acts as a noise filter — the PR team
only receives notifications for items the AI has classified as high risk.
6. Telegram Alert (High Risk Branch)
Sends an immediate push notification to a configured Telegram chat:
🚨 Alerta PR | Riesgo ALTO
[AI-generated summary]
Telegram was chosen over email for its push notification behavior and near-zero
latency — critical for crisis scenarios where response time matters.
7. Odoo — Create Note (Parallel Branch)
Simultaneously creates a note in Odoo CRM with the AI-generated summary. This
ensures every high-risk mention is documented in the client's existing system of
record, regardless of whether the Telegram alert is seen or acted upon.
Key design decision: Both branches run in parallel, not sequentially. The CRM
record is created whether or not the Telegram message is delivered — decoupling
the alert from the audit trail.

Per-Client Customization Points
This workflow has been sold and adapted multiple times. Common adjustments per
deployment:

RSS feed URL — swapped via environment variable, no workflow editing required
Risk classification language — some clients use English (high/medium/low),
others Spanish (alto/medio/bajo); the IF condition and system prompt are adjusted
accordingly
Alert destination — Telegram is the default; some clients prefer WhatsApp
(via Twilio), Slack, or email
CRM integration — Odoo is the default; replaceable with HubSpot, Notion, or
Google Sheets depending on client stack
Monitoring frequency — hourly is standard; high-profile clients during campaigns
or events may run every 15–30 minutes


Key Takeaways

Environment variables for portability — externalizing client-specific values
(RSS_FEED_URL, TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID) makes a single workflow template deployable
across multiple clients with zero structural changes.
Normalize before AI — always standardize data structure before sending to an
LLM. Inconsistent input produces inconsistent output; a normalization function node
is cheap insurance.
Enforce JSON output from OpenAI — for workflows where AI output feeds into
conditional logic, use responseFormat: json and define the exact fields in the
system prompt. This eliminates parsing errors and makes the IF condition reliable.
Model cost vs. capability matching — GPT-4o-mini is the right choice for
classification tasks running at high frequency. Reserving GPT-4o for tasks that
genuinely require it keeps operational costs sustainable.
Parallel output branches — separating the alert (Telegram) from the audit trail
(Odoo) into parallel branches ensures documentation is never dependent on
notification delivery. Both always run independently.
